WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:08.600
In the last video, you saw one visualization of the cost function j of w or j of wb.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:13.760
Let's look at some further richer visualizations so that you can get an even better intuition

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.600
about what the cost function is doing.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.720
Here is what we've seen so far.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:25.880
There's the model, the model's parameters w and b, the cost function j of w and b,

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:32.520
as well as the goal of linear regression, which is to minimize the cost function j of w and b

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:35.560
over parameters w and b.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:42.240
In the last video, we had temporarily set b to 0 in order to simplify the visualizations.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:48.520
But now let's go back to the original model with both parameters w and b without setting

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.680
b to be equal to 0.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:57.400
Same as last time, we want to get a visual understanding of the model function f of x,

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:05.160
shown here on the left, and how it relates to the cost function j of wb, shown here on

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:06.160
the right.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:10.760
Here's a training set of house sizes and prices.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:14.960
Let's say you pick one possible function of x, like this one.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:20.480
Here I've set w to 0.06 and b to 50.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:25.800
So f of x is 0.06 times x plus 50.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.560
Note that this is not a particularly good model for this training set.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.440
It's actually a pretty bad model.

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:35.280
It seems to consistently underestimate housing prices.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:41.480
Given these values for w and b, let's look at what the cost function j of w and b may

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:42.760
look like.

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:49.560
Recall what we saw last time was when you had only w, because we temporarily set b

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.200
to 0 to simplify things.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:57.880
Back then, we had come up with a plot of the cost function that looked like this as a function

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:00.360
of w only.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:06.960
So when we had only one parameter w, the cost function had this u-shaped curve, shaped a

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.840
bit like a soup bowl.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.240
That sounds delicious.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:18.680
Now in this housing price example that we have on this slide, we have two parameters, w

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:24.080
and b, and so the plot becomes a little more complex.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:31.960
It turns out that the cost function also has a similar shape, like a soup bowl, except

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:34.920
in three dimensions instead of two.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:40.840
In fact, depending on your training set, the cost function will look something like this.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:45.480
To me, this looks like a soup bowl, maybe because I'm a little bit hungry.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:50.840
Or maybe to you, it looks like a curved dinner plate, or a hammock.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:55.880
Actually that sounds relaxing too, and there's your coconut drink.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:59.840
Maybe when you're done with this course, you should treat yourself to vacation and relax

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:01.680
in a hammock like this.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:08.800
What you see here is a 3D surface plot where the axes are labeled w and b.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:14.440
So as you vary w and b, which are the two parameters of the model, you get different

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:19.160
values for the cost function j of w and b.

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:24.360
This is a lot like the u-shaped curve you saw in the last video, except instead of having

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:31.880
one parameter, w as input in the j, you now have two parameters, w and b, as inputs into

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:35.720
this soup bowl or this hammock shaped function j.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:40.440
And I just want to point out that any single point on this surface represents a particular

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.640
choice of w and b.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:52.240
For example, if w was minus 10 and b was minus 15, then the height of the surface above this

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:59.960
point is the value of j when w is minus 10 and b is minus 15.

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:05.840
Now in order to look even more closely at specific points, there's another way of plotting

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:11.680
the cost function j that would be useful for visualization, which is rather than using

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:17.440
these 3D surface plots, I'd like to take this exact same function j, so I'm not changing

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:23.280
the function j at all, and plot it using something called a contour plot.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:28.560
And if you've ever seen a topographical map showing how high different mountains are,

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:35.480
the contours in a topographical map are basically horizontal slices of the landscape of say

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:36.840
a mountain.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:40.320
This image is of Mount Fuji in Japan.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:45.880
I still remember my family visiting Mount Fuji when I was a teenager with some beautiful

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:47.760
sights.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:53.960
And if you fly directly above the mountain, that's what this contour map looks like.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:59.960
It shows all the points that are at the same height for different heights.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:05.200
At the bottom of this slide is the 3D surface plot of the cost function j.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:10.840
I know it doesn't look very bow shaped, but it is actually a bow just very stretched

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:13.120
out which is why it looks like that.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:18.800
In an optional map that is shortly to follow, you'll be able to see this in 3D and spin

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:23.480
around to surface yourself and it'll look more obviously bow shaped there.

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:30.000
Next, here on the upper right is a contour plot of this exact same cost function as

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.560
that shown at the bottom.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:41.560
The two axes on this contour plot are b on the vertical axis and w on the horizontal axis.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:49.760
What each of these ovals also called ellipses shows is the set of points on the 3D surface

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:52.040
which are at the exact same height.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:58.160
In other words, the set of points which have the same value for the cost function j.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:05.840
So to get the contour plot, you take the 3D surface at the bottom and you use a knife

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:08.520
to slice it horizontally.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:14.240
You take horizontal slices of that 3D surface and get all the points that are at the same

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:15.240
height.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:22.600
Therefore, each horizontal slice ends up being shown as one of these ellipses or one of

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:25.040
these ovals.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:34.000
So concretely, if you take that point and that point and that point, all of these three

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:39.920
points have the same value for the cost function j even though they have different

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:43.920
values for w and b.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:49.920
And in the figure on the upper left, you see also that these three points correspond

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:56.360
to different functions f, all three of which are actually pretty bad for predicting housing

00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:58.600
prices in this case.

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:07.240
Now the bottom of the boat where the cost function j is at a minimum is this point

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:12.000
right here at the center of this concentric ovals.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.040
If you haven't seen contour plots much before, I'd like you to imagine if you

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:24.320
will that you are flying high up above the bow in an aeroplane or in a rocket ship and

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:26.760
you're looking straight down at it.

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:33.360
So that is as if you set your computer monitor flat on your desk facing up and the bow

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:38.440
shape is coming directly out of your screen rising above your desk.

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:45.360
Imagine that the bow shape grows out of your computer screen line flat like that so that

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:52.160
each of these ovals have the same height above your screen and the minimum of the bow is

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:57.000
right down there in the center of the smallest oval.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:02.760
So it turns out that the contour plots are a convenient way to visualize the 3D cost

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:07.880
function j but in a way that's plotted in just 2D.

00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:14.360
In this video, you saw how the 3D bow shaped surface plot can also be visualized as a

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:16.400
contour plot.

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:22.120
Using this visualization tool, in the next video, let's visualize some specific choices

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:27.880
of WMB in the linear regression model so that we can see how these different choices affect

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:30.600
the straight line you're fitting to the data.

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:32.080
Let's go on to the next video.
